==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abusioun}}, from {{der|en|fro|abusion}}, from {{der|en|la|abūsiō||abuse, misuse}}, from {{m|la|abūtor||misuse}}.<ref name=WI3>{{R:MW3 1976|page=8}}</ref> {{doublet|en|abusio}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbjuː.ʒn̩/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abusion.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbju.ʒn̩/}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːʒən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{tlb|en|chiefly|EME}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[misuse|Misuse]], [[abuse]]; in particular, [[illegal]] behaviour; verbal, physical or sexual abuse. {{defdate|14th–18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[deceit|Deceit]]; abuse of the [[truth]]. {{defdate|15th–17th c.}}
#* {{c.|1515–1516}}, published 1568, {{w|John Skelton}}, ''Againſt venemous tongues enpoyſoned with ſclaunder and falſe detractions &c.'':
#*: Such tunges unhappy hath made great diviſion<br>In realmes, in cities, by ſuche fals '''abuſion''';<br>Of fals fickil tunges ſuche cloked colluſion<br>Hath brought nobil princes to extreme confuſion.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=XI|passage={{...}} by those vgly formes weren pourtrayd, / Foolish delights and fond '''abusions''', / Which do that sence besiege with light illusions.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|rhetoric}} [[catachresis|Catachresis]]. {{defdate|16th–18th c.}}

